Enterprise Campus Network with High Availability
Overview

This project is a Cisco Packet Tracer enterprise networking homelab designed to simulate the deployment of a modern campus network for a small-to-medium business.

The objective is to apply CCNA networking concepts in a realistic enterprise environment by designing, implementing, securing, and documenting a highly available network infrastructure.

Rather than configuring isolated labs, this project follows the workflow of a real network deployment—from planning and network design to implementation, testing, and documentation.

Project Objectives
Design an enterprise campus network using industry best practices.
Implement network segmentation using VLANs.
Configure redundant core infrastructure for high availability.
Enable secure communication between departments.
Implement dynamic routing and gateway redundancy.
Apply network security and device hardening techniques.
Document the complete implementation process.
Planned Network Topology

The enterprise network consists of:

1 Edge Router
2 Layer 3 Core Switches
3 Access Switches
IT Department
Human Resources Department
Accounting Department
9 Workstations
3 Network Printers
Multiple Servers (DHCP, DNS, File Server)

The network is designed with redundant uplinks between the core and access layers to provide fault tolerance and continuous connectivity.

Technologies Implemented

This project will demonstrate the following networking technologies:

VLANs
802.1Q Trunking
Inter-VLAN Routing
OSPF
HSRP
EtherChannel (LACP)
Rapid PVST+
Access Control Lists (ACLs)
DHCP
NAT/PAT
SSH
Port Security
Network Device Hardening
Syslog
NTP
